GLA UniversitY2nd Mid Term ExamB. Tech: II Year, Ssemester [C.S, E.C. IEthics & Values (AHE: 201)
Marks: 20 Time: l'30 HoursThis paper consists of two passages. Carefully read the passages and answer the following multiplo choicequestions. All questions carry equal marks, i.e. I mark each.
I
Passage: IWhat is the most demanding wish other thanfreedom? Indeed, it is respect for individuals'freedom. Theories of individual rights make thepoint clear that each individual has a separatelife and different preferences. Individuals'preferences should not be sacrificed for eitherlarger social purposes or the common good. Wepeople not only desire. but also value thefreedom. By the very human nature we likefreedom .and do not want to live in therestrictions of laws and rules. Nevertheless, wedesire those minimum laws and rules whichactually protect ourselves from others.Libertarians define freedom in such a way thatfreedom for individuals is all or nothing' In otherwords, they argue individuals must be givencomplete freedom' Howevero Libertariansdifferentiate between individual and collective(social) freedom. According to libertarians, thereshould not be any kind of limitations onindividual freedom. However, there is acondition for eollective freedom that one is freeto the extent that one must not harm the other.Most interesting feature of the Libertarians' ideaof freedom is that one has got complete freedomin a very precise sense that one can' if onewishes so, harrn himself or herself. Libertariansadvocate for the minimal state theory.What this theory simply means is that stateshould not limit the liberty of individuals or agovernment should not regulate the individuals'clioices and actions by propounding certainrestrictions and laws. Individuals have got
fundamental right to liberty. Government has gotonly role to play in individuals' lives, and that isto protect them from any kind of external threatlike theft fraud, and comrption. Libertariansobject the excessiv€ use of the powers ofgovernment in .the way it interuene inindividuals' lives and coerce them to followcertain laws. There are three such objections byLibertarians against the governments. First,govemment should not make paternalistic laws'Second, government should not make laws forthe morals legislation of individuals' lives'Third, government should not redistributewealth by putting taxes on individuals' salary'By paternalistic laws we mean suoh laws whichare actually for the individuals' safety andprotection like the helmet, seatbelt driving safetylaws, and the pension laws. Libertarians arguethat enforcing such laws on an individual agovernment undermines his or her risk takingability. Libertarians believe that governmentshould not propose a certain kind of values orprinciples for individuals' lives. An individualmust be given full freedom to choose his or herown pafierns and ways to live the life' However,Libertarians put one condition on this that one'smoral choices must not affect the others' On theissue of taxation, Libertarians contend thegovernment and say: by putting taxes onindividuals' income government takes away theownership of an individual. The amount we payas taxes becomes slavery for an individual' Whatwe earn through our labor talent or skill is ourself expression. In this way, a self becomes slaveand one's labor becomes forced labor' This is
