personal decision if it does notharm any other.herself.Passage:2Immanuel Kanl the father of human rights,explains the ideas of individual .freedom andrespect for human beings in so neat andscientific manner. Being contemporary of SirIsaac Newton, Kant was very much influencedwith Newtonian physics, and he was in fullagreement with Sir Newton on the point thatnature is governed by the deterministic physicallaws. However,.this determinism was a sort ofchallenge for Kant to defure, that if we aregoverned by the deterministic laws ilten, in whatsense we can be said to be free' Kant byrespecting the Newtonian' Physics initiates hisargument in such a beautiful manner and saysthat there are two kinds of world: l) Physical,and 2) Moral. Further, he argues that we peopleare governed by the physical laws rn so far as weoperate in nature (we operate under law ofgravitation, in a certain atmosphere consisting ofdensrty, pressure and other physical conditions)'However, Kant argues this could be a limitationon the animal life because there exists the oneand only physical world for them. But, forindividual persons, there exists what Kant refersto as moral world. Moral world is such a worldthat we create for ourselves. And, we can createit by the virtue of human rationality. Moralworld is a world of rationality in which thehuman life is governed by reasons. Kant says itis only human beings who have got mind and itsfaculties of thinking and reasoning. By havingrecourse to these faculties of mind,.an individualperson can intend, resist and oan do certain actsand can refrain herselfltrimself from doingsomething. Kant argues that a person is free, and
