free by reason. For example, let's compare twopersons one is guided by reason and anotherwho does not use 'rationality. You can easilyunderstand that the one.who follows the reasoncan explain many events of life but who does notfollow would be depending either on the other orwould be always operating under certain fear orexternal command. This is the reason why Kantargues that it is rationality through which we getthe freedom. Thus, Kantian idea of individualfreedom, unlike the Libertarians, is grounded in .human rationality. But Kantianism andLibertarians unanimously argue that individualchoice should not be sacrificed for larger socialpurposes. Establishing the idea of individualfreedom Kant goes ahead to explain the idea ofrespect for the human beings. Despite, humanbeings possess rationality and autonomy theymust be respected because they have thecapacrty to legislate morality for themselves.They can create morbl rules for their own lives.This is what Kant calls self-legislation. In othprworlds, we can say one c'an realize the sense offreedom in creating good rules for one'sbetterment of life.Kant further tries to explain that morality shouldbe universal and uniform, and should not dependeither on personal biasness or external pressure.Morality must be objectively rational in itscharacter that we people can easily understand itwithout involving the personal reasons, and ifwe really want to include our personal reasonsthen we must not personalize the reasons butrationalize them to the extent that they becomeunbiased, universal .and uniform. Kani afterexplaining the ideas of rationality and freedomfurther moves to pxplain the idea of moralaccountabilig and says that we people can beheld responsible once we act freely andrationally. However, he makes a very interestingpoint that it is very much possible that a persondoes something .X' and .X' is right and s/treknows very well that .X' is right but there is
possibility that 'X' is not moral. Reallyinteresting moral fact it is, Kant says that. aperson who does 'X' but not by his or her moralchoice. The person may not have chosen thataction or Vhe was not free to do any otheraction. It means the person has performed theaction either on the other's choice or undercoercion, pressure or force. The action may beright but in Kantian defiriition it is not moral.So, you do something right but if not on yourown choice then your action does not have anymoral worth. It cannot be said morally apraiseworthy action. Thus, we can praise orblame someone for his or her actions only in thatsomeone has performed an action with freedomand reason. Kant says moral responsibility canbe ascribed to someone who is both a free andrational creature. It is in this sense Kantemphasizes that morality should be free fromreligion. Kant says morality is an expression ofoneself. Morality must freely evolve in oneself.Kantian description of morality is based on thedeontolory.. Deontological morality is aframework in which mordl worth of an action,duty, rule, decision or p€rson lies in the verynature of the action, rule, duty, decision orperson.Unlike, Consequentialism, Kantianism focuseson the means, ways, or the procedures we followto perform an action. This view holds we shouldonly care the actions not the consequences. Weshould be careful to the means that we choose rofulfill an end and there is no need to look at theends. Kant says that even if your right means donot produce the right end do not get depressed asyour action carry a moral worth within itself.For example, suppose if someone being studenthonestly study hard by. practicing his dutysomehow does not get good marks, then thisstudent, according to Kan! need not be worriedand disappointed in so far as his action ismorally correct and good. Kant put it in this way"Duty for the sake of duty." Kant maintains
