universalify in his morality by explaining theidea of categorical imperative. He argues in allmoral rules there is an imperative but it can beof two kinds: J) Hypothetical, and 2)Categorical. Hypothetical imperative means theimperative or moral command of an actiondepends on external conditions. For example, ifyou want to get good marks then study hard. Inthis example for a stude.nt studding hard is'amorally correct thing but it has to qualiff acondition, and that is to get the good marks.Categorical imperative is supreme principle ofmorality. However, categorical imperativemeans the imperative or moral command of anaction is unconditional. For example, do yourduty. Kant argues the moral worth of a moralaction does not depend on the consequences itproduces but depends on our intentions behindour actions. For example, a murderer and adoctor both uses a knife but with differentintentions one uses to kill a life and other uses tosave a life. Here Kant introduces his beautifulidea of good- will and says that no action can besaid to have moral worth.unless performed by'asense of good will. What is good-will? Kantreplies this question so neatly and says that awill is different from desire. Desires arepsychological and represent our emotions andpassions. And, they lack determination that iswhy our desires get changing all the time.However, will is not purely psychological, it isbased on reasons and have got determination aswell. Kant says, although will is rational andhas got strong determination but without beinggood a normal will may carry wrong intentions.For example; a sfrong willed person wants to usehis determination in killing someone. This is thereason why Kant argues that our will mustfollow good intentions that's what he calls good-will. He says without good-will morality cannotbe performed and all values say knowledge,power and beauty can be misused. For example,a knowledgeable person can misuse theknowledge for the wrong purposes making cheat
and frauds, powerful person oan misuse thepower by beating a week. Without having good-will we cannot act morally.Questions:1) A person oP' performs an action 'X'.And, 'X' is right. And, P knows ttrat 'X'is right. But, his action 'X' cannot besaid to be moral because:2) Kant argues that the moral world is:3) Kant says human beings are freebecause:can practice reasons.freedom.4) Kant argues we should respect allhuman beings:respected.themselves.other thingmorality.5) Someone can be held morallYresponsible for an action onlY if :her own will.6) According to Kant morality is:reasons.
